# JSON
@summary Stdlib Json — parseJson, toJson, toJsonPretty
@category stdlib

The Json module converts between JSON text and SPN values. It's intentionally minimal — three functions cover serialization in both directions.

## Mapping between JSON and SPN

When parseJson reads a JSON value, it produces SPN values according to this mapping:

JSON object → Dict with symbol keys (so the key for "name" is :name)
JSON array → Array
JSON string → string
JSON integer → Long
JSON float → Double
JSON true/false → Boolean
JSON null → None (the empty case of Option)

When toJson/toJsonPretty writes a value, the same mapping applies in reverse. Symbol keys in dicts become JSON object keys.

## Functions

parseJson(json) — parse a JSON string and return the SPN value. Throws on malformed input.

toJson(value) — serialize to a compact one-line JSON string.

toJsonPretty(value) — serialize with indentation and line breaks for human reading.

## Symbol keys

Because JSON object keys map to SPN symbol keys, you read fields by symbol literal, not by string:

dictGet(parsed, :name)   -- correct
dictGet(parsed, "name")  -- wrong: would look up the string key "name"

This makes JSON-derived dicts ergonomic with the symbol/atom syntax used elsewhere in SPN (:click events, ADT discriminants, etc.).

--- example: parse-and-read.spn
-- Parse a JSON object and read fields with symbol keys.
import Json (parseJson)
import Dict (dictGet)

let raw = "{\"name\": \"alice\", \"age\": 30, \"admin\": true}"
let parsed = parseJson(raw)

let name = dictGet(parsed, :name)
let age = dictGet(parsed, :age)
let admin = dictGet(parsed, :admin)

(name, age, admin)
-- ("alice", 30, true)
---

--- example: round-trip.spn
-- Build a value, serialize, parse back, compare.
import Json (toJson, parseJson)
import Dict (emptyDict, put, dictGet)

let original = put(put(put(emptyDict(),
    :id,    7),
    :name,  "widget"),
    :tags,  ["red", "small"])

let text = toJson(original)
let restored = parseJson(text)

let sameId = dictGet(restored, :id) == dictGet(original, :id)
sameId
-- true
---

--- example: pretty-config.spn
-- Pretty-print a config dict for human-readable output to a file.
import Json (toJsonPretty)
import Dict (emptyDict, put)
import File (writeFile)

let config = emptyDict()
config = put(config, :host, "localhost")
config = put(config, :port, 8080)
config = put(config, :flags, ["verbose", "trace"])

let text = toJsonPretty(config)
writeFile("/tmp/config.json", text)
---

--- example: parse-array.spn
-- The top-level JSON value can be any type. Here we parse an array
-- of records and project a single field from each.
import Json (parseJson)
import Array (map, length)
import Dict (dictGet)

let raw = "[{\"x\":1,\"y\":2},{\"x\":3,\"y\":4},{\"x\":5,\"y\":6}]"
let points = parseJson(raw)

-- Pull the x coordinate out of each record.
let xs = map(points, do(p) { dictGet(p, :x) })
xs
-- [1, 3, 5]
---
